<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">F. W. de Klerk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Frederik Willem de Klerk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">F. W. de Klerk was the last state president of apartheid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, serving from 1989 to 1994. In February 1990 he unbanned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> and other liberation movements and released <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, and he negotiated the end of white minority rule. He shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> with Mandela in 1993 and served as a deputy president in Mandela's government until 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk.jpg" alt="F. W. de Klerk" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carol M. Highsmith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F.W._de_Klerk_wait_to_speak_in_Philadelphia,_Pennsylvania_LCCN2011634246_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederik Willem de Klerk was born on 18 March 1936 in Mayfair, a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, into an influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> family with long ties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>. His father Jan de Klerk was a senator, president of the senate for seven years, acting state president and a cabinet member for fifteen years. His aunt's husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-g-strijdom">J. G. Strijdom</a>, was a former prime minister, and his brother Willem later left the party to found the liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a>. De Klerk studied at Potchefstroom University from 1954 to 1958, took degrees in arts and law, and was initiated into the Broederbond. He set up a law partnership in Vereeniging in 1962. In 1972 he was elected to the House of Assembly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As a minister in the government of P. W. Botha, de Klerk supported and enforced apartheid. After Botha resigned as party leader, de Klerk defeated Barend du Plessis, 69 votes to 61, and was elected leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> on 2 February 1989. Botha resigned the presidency on 14 August 1989, and de Klerk was elected State President on 20 September. ANC leaders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu">Desmond Tutu</a> doubted that he would differ from his predecessors, but he allowed a protest march of about 30,000 people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> to proceed, saying the door to a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was open.</p><p>His presidency was dominated by negotiations between his government and the ANC. In a whites-only referendum on 17 March 1992 more than two thirds voted to continue negotiating an end to apartheid. State security forces committed widespread human rights abuses and encouraged violence between Xhosa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> people, though de Klerk later denied sanctioning such actions. Mandela distrusted de Klerk's role, particularly regarding the so-called third force, and the question was never clarified. In 1993 de Klerk publicly apologised for apartheid's harmful effects. After the 1994 election he served as one of two deputy presidents, and he opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-south-africa">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a>, preferring a blanket amnesty. In May 1996 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> withdrew from the coalition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>De Klerk retired from active politics in 1997 and lectured internationally. His autobiography The Last Trek appeared in 1999. He died on 11 November 2021 at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, a few months after a diagnosis of mesothelioma. Afterwards the F. W. de Klerk Foundation released a video in which he apologised without qualification for the harm caused by apartheid. He was a controversial figure. Anti-apartheid activists criticised him for offering only a qualified apology and for ignoring abuses by the security forces, while supporters of apartheid held that he had betrayed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> minority. He himself said that his views had changed completely, as in a conversion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “F. W. de Klerk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>F. W. de Klerk</b>, president from 1989, unbanned the ANC and freed Nelson Mandela in 1990, and negotiations produced an interim constitution and the first democratic elections on 27 April 1994.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…in secret from 1985, and after the end of the Cold War had weakened the government’s claim to be a bulwark against communism, Botha’s successor <b>F. W. de Klerk</b> unbanned the ANC, the PAC and the Communist Party on 2 February 1990; Mandela walked free on 11 February.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-26.2,28.04&amp;year=1991" aria-label="Johannesburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of F. W. de Klerk"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4850 4677h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4850 4677h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-26.2,28.04&amp;year=1991">What was here in 1991? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
